{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's claim that the cross-city nocturnal UHI gap depends 'mainly on sky view factor' presents sky view as the single dominant cross-city modulator, but the problem asks for 'what single mechanism best explains why that night-time gap is larger in some cities than in others.' Sky view factor is a geometric parameter, not a mechanism: it modulates the rate of radiative cooling but does not itself account for which cities have more heat available to trap. Denser/taller cities with low sky view also typically have greater anthropogenic heat flux and more thermal mass, so the argument that sky view is the 'main' driver confuses a necessary radiative constraint with the sufficient mechanism. The target offers no evidence isolating sky view from co-varying factors, so its identification of the 'single mechanism' is under-justified.","counterexample":[{"city":"Phoenix, AZ (low building height, high sky view factor) vs. Boston, MA (taller, narrow streets, low sky view factor)","observation":"Phoenix exhibits a very large nocturnal UHI despite high sky view factor because aridity, low thermal inertia of desert surroundings, and anthropogenic heat dominate, while Boston's nocturnal UHI can be smaller or comparable despite far lower sky view","implication":"If low sky view were the main cross-city mechanism, Phoenix would have a small nocturnal gap and Boston a large one; the observed pattern contradicts the claimed dominance of sky view factor"}],"premise":null,"target_alias":"SRC_001"}]}